# Section 8: Rule of 3, Rule of 5, and Rule of 0 (Comprehensive Chapter)

> **Why This Chapter Matters:** Memory leaks, double-free crashes, and accidental slow deep copies are the #1 C++ interview filter. Mastering Rule of 3 / 5 / 0 gives you complete command over object lifetime and resource ownership.

---

## 1. Core Concepts Explained Simply

### Why Do These Rules Exist?
When your class only contains plain values (`int`, `double`, `std::string`), the C++ compiler's **implicitly generated** destructor, copy constructor, and copy assignment work cleanly.  
**The moment your class manually owns a raw resource** (a heap pointer `new int[]`, a C `FILE*`, a network socket), the compiler's defaults only do **shallow memberwise copy** and **do nothing in the destructor**—causing memory leaks, aliasing bugs, and double-free crashes!

### Resource Ownership & RAII
**RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization)** means:
- **Acquire** the resource in the constructor.
- **Release** the resource automatically in the destructor.
- Every resource must have a crystal-clear **owner** responsible for freeing it exactly once.

### The 5 Special Member Functions (Plus Default Constructor)
1. **Destructor (`~T()`):** Frees owned resources when object dies.
2. **Copy Constructor (`T(const T& other)`):** Builds a brand-new object as an independent deep copy of `other`.
3. **Copy Assignment Operator (`T& operator=(const T& rhs)`):** Replaces an existing object's state with a deep copy of `rhs` (freeing old resource safely, guarding self-assignment).
4. **Move Constructor (`T(T&& other) noexcept`):** Steals resources from an expiring rvalue `other` in $O(1)$ and leaves `other` null/empty so `~T()` won't double-free!
5. **Move Assignment Operator (`T& operator=(T&& rhs) noexcept`):** Releases `*this`'s current resource, steals `rhs`'s resource in $O(1)$, and nulls `rhs`.

---

## 2. Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy & Self-Assignment

- **Shallow Copy:** Copies pointer address (`this->ptr = other.ptr;`). Two objects point to the same heap block -> mutating one corrupts the other, and both destructors `delete` the same address (**Double Free UB**).
- **Deep Copy:** Allocates fresh memory (`new int[n]`) and copies element values -> objects are 100% independent.
- **Self-Assignment (`a = a;`):** If `operator=` calls `delete[] data_;` before checking `if (this == &rhs)`, `a` deletes its *own* buffer before copying from it -> **Use-After-Free UB**!

---

## 3. When to Use Each Rule

| Rule | Exact Statement | When to Use |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Rule of 3** | If you define any of **Destructor**, **Copy Ctor**, or **Copy Assign**, define **all 3**. | Legacy C++98/03 code or simple raw-resource classes where move isn't needed (though in C++11+ prefer Rule of 5!). |
| **Rule of 5** | If you define any of the 5 special functions, explicitly define (or `= default` / `= delete`) **all 5**. | Low-level RAII wrappers (`UniqueFile`, custom `Vector`, `SmartPtr`) or when writing a custom destructor in a base class. |
| **Rule of 0** | Define **zero** custom destructors, copy ops, or move ops; compose RAII types (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::vector`, `std::string`). | **95%+ of all modern C++17 classes!** Zero boilerplate, zero leaks, automatic `noexcept` moves! |

### Why Modern C++ Strongly Prefers Rule of 0
1. **Zero manual `new`/`delete` bugs:** `std::vector` and `std::unique_ptr` are already battle-tested.
2. **Single Responsibility Principle:** Your `Student` or `Invoice` class focuses on business logic, not heap pointer bookkeeping.
3. **Automatic `noexcept` Move Semantics:** Writing an empty `~Student() {}` silently **disables** the implicit move constructor! Rule of 0 keeps fast moves enabled automatically.

---

## 4. Complete Implementations (Side-by-Side)

### A. Rule of 3 Implementation (Deep-Copy `IntArray`)
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>

class IntArray3 {
    std::size_t size_;
    int* data_;
public:
    explicit IntArray3(std::size_t n = 0)
        : size_(n), data_(n ? new int[n]() : nullptr) {}

    ~IntArray3() { delete[] data_; } // 1. Destructor

    IntArray3(const IntArray3& o)    // 2. Copy Constructor
        : size_(o.size_), data_(o.size_ ? new int[o.size_] : nullptr) {
        std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + size_, data_);
    }

    IntArray3& operator=(const IntArray3& rhs) { // 3. Copy Assignment
        if (this == &rhs) return *this;
        int* fresh = rhs.size_ ? new int[rhs.size_] : nullptr;
        std::copy(rhs.data_, rhs.data_ + rhs.size_, fresh);
        delete[] data_;
        data_ = fresh;
        size_ = rhs.size_;
        return *this;
    }
};
```

### B. Rule of 5 Implementation (`IntArray5` with `noexcept` Moves)
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <utility>

class IntArray5 {
    std::size_t size_;
    int* data_;
public:
    explicit IntArray5(std::size_t n = 0)
        : size_(n), data_(n ? new int[n]() : nullptr) {}

    ~IntArray5() { delete[] data_; } // 1. Destructor

    IntArray5(const IntArray5& o)    // 2. Copy Constructor
        : size_(o.size_), data_(o.size_ ? new int[o.size_] : nullptr) {
        std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + size_, data_);
    }

    IntArray5& operator=(const IntArray5& rhs) { // 3. Copy Assignment
        if (this == &rhs) return *this;
        int* fresh = rhs.size_ ? new int[rhs.size_] : nullptr;
        std::copy(rhs.data_, rhs.data_ + rhs.size_, fresh);
        delete[] data_;
        data_ = fresh;
        size_ = rhs.size_;
        return *this;
    }

    IntArray5(IntArray5&& o) noexcept // 4. Move Constructor
        : size_(std::exchange(o.size_, 0)), data_(std::exchange(o.data_, nullptr)) {}

    IntArray5& operator=(IntArray5&& rhs) noexcept { // 5. Move Assignment
        if (this != &rhs) {
            delete[] data_;
            data_ = std::exchange(rhs.data_, nullptr);
            size_ = std::exchange(rhs.size_, 0);
        }
        return *this;
    }
};
```

### C. Rule of 0 Implementation (`IntArray0` — Idiomatic Modern C++17)
```cpp
#include <vector>

class IntArray0 {
    std::vector<int> data_;
public:
    explicit IntArray0(std::size_t n = 0) : data_(n, 0) {}
    // Destructor, copy ctor, copy assign, move ctor, move assign all auto-generated!
    std::size_t size() const noexcept { return data_.size(); }
    int& operator[](std::size_t i) { return data_[i]; }
};
```

---

## 5. Common Mistakes
1. **Writing `~T() = default;` in a Rule of 0 class:** Suppresses implicit move constructor & move assignment! (Exception: polymorphic base classes need `virtual ~Base() = default;`, and should usually `= delete` copy/move or `= default` all 5.)
2. **Forgetting `noexcept` on Move Operations:** Causes `std::vector::push_back` reallocations to copy instead of move!
3. **Leaving moved-from pointer un-nulled:** Causes double free when the moved-from temporary is destroyed at `;`.
4. **Calling `std::move` on a `const` object (`const IntArray5 a; IntArray5 b = std::move(a);`):** `std::move(a)` yields `const IntArray5&&`, which **cannot** bind to `IntArray5&&`—silently falling back to the **Copy Constructor**!

---

## 6. Tricky Interview Questions (Rule of 3/5/0)
1. **If I write ONLY a Move Constructor `T(T&&)`, what happens to Copy Constructor & Copy Assignment?**  
   *Answer:* Both are **implicitly `= delete`d**! Your class becomes move-only automatically.
2. **If I write ONLY a Copy Constructor `T(const T&)`, does the compiler generate a Move Constructor?**  
   *Answer:* **No!** The compiler refuses to generate move operations if any copy operation or destructor is user-declared.
3. **What is the "Rule of 4.5" (Copy-and-Swap Unified Assignment)?**  
   *Answer:* Write Destructor, Copy Ctor, Move Ctor, and ONE unified `T& operator=(T other) noexcept { swap(*this, other); return *this; }`—covering both copy and move assignment in 4 lines!
4. **Can a moved-from object still be assigned a new value later?**  
   *Answer:* **Yes!** A moved-from object must stay in a valid (though unspecified) state so both `~T()` and `a = newValue;` work safely.
5. **Why does `std::unique_ptr` obey Rule of 0 inside caller classes, while itself being implemented with Rule of 5?**  
   *Answer:* `std::unique_ptr` is the low-level RAII primitive (Rule of 5: move-only), enabling every class *using* it to follow Rule of 0!

---

## 7. Output-Based Questions (Rule of 3/5/0)

### Output Q1: Move Fallback on `const` Object
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

struct S {
    S() = default;
    S(const S&) { std::cout << "Copy "; }
    S(S&&) noexcept { std::cout << "Move "; }
};

int main() {
    const S a;
    S b = std::move(a);
}
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer</summary>

- **Output:** `Copy `
- **Why:** `std::move(a)` casts `const S` to `const S&&`. Since `S(S&&)` needs non-const `S&&`, overload resolution falls back to `S(const S&)`!
</details>

### Output Q2: Implicit Copy Suppression by Move Constructor
```cpp
#include <iostream>

struct MoveOnly {
    MoveOnly() = default;
    MoveOnly(MoveOnly&&) noexcept { std::cout << "Moved\n"; }
};

int main() {
    MoveOnly m1;
    // MoveOnly m2 = m1; // <-- Compile Error or Copy?
    MoveOnly m3 = static_cast<MoveOnly&&>(m1);
}
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer</summary>

- **Output:** `Moved` (and uncommenting `MoveOnly m2 = m1;` causes a **COMPILE ERROR** because declaring `MoveOnly(MoveOnly&&)` implicitly deleted the copy constructor!)
</details>

---

## 8. Coding Exercises (Rule of 3/5/0)

### Exercise 1: Rule of 5 `DynText` with `std::exchange`
Implement `DynText` owning a `char*` buffer supporting all 5 Rule-of-5 functions safely (including `nullptr` handling and self-move safety).  
*Key Pattern:* Allocate `new char[len + 1]` on copy; `std::exchange(other.buf_, nullptr)` on move; `delete[] buf_` in `~DynText()`.

### Exercise 2: Rule of 0 Polymorphic `DocumentBundle`
Create a `DocumentBundle` holding `std::vector<std::shared_ptr<const Document>>` that is freely copyable, movable, and leak-free with **zero** custom special member functions.  
*Key Pattern:* `struct DocumentBundle { std::string title; std::vector<std::shared_ptr<const Document>> docs; };` — all 5 special functions auto-generated cleanly!

