# Section 9: Copy Assignment Operator (Zero to Interview Mastery)

> **Why This Chapter Matters:** Copy Assignment (`operator=`) was on your pending syllabus—and it's one of the most heavily tested C++ interview topics because getting `operator=` wrong silently corrupts memory, leaks resources, or crashes on self-assignment.

---

## 1. What Is Copy Assignment, and When Is It Called?

The **copy assignment operator** replaces the contents of an **already-constructed** object with a copy of another object of the same class.

### Canonical Syntax
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass& operator=(const MyClass& rhs); // "rhs" = right-hand side
};
```

### When Is It Called vs Copy Constructor?
```cpp
MyClass a(10);      // Parameterized constructor
MyClass b = a;      // COPY CONSTRUCTOR! ('b' is being born right now)
MyClass c(20);      // Parameterized constructor
c = a;              // COPY ASSIGNMENT! ('c' ALREADY existed before this line)
c.operator=(a);     // Equivalent explicit call
```

### Difference from Copy Constructor & Move Assignment
| Feature | Copy Constructor | Copy Assignment (`operator=`) | Move Assignment (`operator=(T&&)`) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Target Object State** | Uninitialized raw memory | Already alive (holds old state/resources) | Already alive (holds old state/resources) |
| **Source (`rhs`)** | `const T&` (unchanged) | `const T&` (unchanged) | `T&&` (resources stolen; left empty/valid) |
| **Cleanup Old State?** | No | **Yes!** Must free/replace old buffers | **Yes!** Must free/replace old buffers |
| **Self-check needed?** | No | **Yes** (`this == &rhs` or copy-and-swap) | **Yes** (`this != &rhs`) |
| **Return Value** | None | `T&` (`return *this;`) | `T&` (`return *this;`) |

---

## 2. Returning `*this` by Reference (`T&`)

1. **Why return `*this`?**  
   To support **chained assignment** like `a = b = c;`. Assignment in C++ is **right-to-left associative**, meaning `a = b = c;` parses as:
   `a.operator=(b.operator=(c));`  
   If `operator=` returned `void`, `b.operator=(c)` would return nothing and `a = ...` would fail to compile!
2. **Why return a reference `T&` instead of `T` by value or `const T&`?**
   - Returning `T` by value would construct and destroy a pointless temporary copy on every assignment.
   - Returning `T&` (non-const) matches built-in `int` semantics where `(a = b) = c;` and `funcTakingRef(a = b)` compile cleanly.

---

## 3. Compiler-Generated Copy Assignment & Raw Pointers

If you don't declare a copy assignment operator (and haven't declared move ops), the compiler generates one automatically that assigns each base and non-static member in order:
```cpp
// What the compiler generates for `class Box { int w; int* p; };`:
Box& operator=(const Box& rhs) {
    w = rhs.w;
    p = rhs.p; // SHALLOW POINTER COPY!
    return *this;
}
```
**What goes wrong when a class contains an owning raw pointer `int* p;`?**
1. **Memory Leak:** `b`'s old `p` address is overwritten and lost forever (never `delete`d!).
2. **Aliasing:** Both `a.p` and `b.p` now point to the same heap block.
3. **Double Free (UB):** When `a` and `b` leave scope, both destructors `delete` the exact same pointer!

---

## 4. Deep Copy, Self-Assignment, Exception Safety & Rule of 3/5/0

### Pattern 1: Classic Safe Deep Copy (`operator=`)
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

class IntBuffer {
    std::size_t size_;
    int* data_;

public:
    explicit IntBuffer(std::size_t n = 0, int val = 0)
        : size_(n), data_(n ? new int[n] : nullptr) {
        std::fill(data_, data_ + size_, val);
    }

    IntBuffer(const IntBuffer& other)
        : size_(other.size_), data_(other.size_ ? new int[other.size_] : nullptr) {
        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);
    }

    // Exception-safe, self-assignment-safe Copy Assignment:
    IntBuffer& operator=(const IntBuffer& rhs) {
        if (this == &rhs) return *this; // 1. Self-assignment guard!

        int* fresh = rhs.size_ ? new int[rhs.size_] : nullptr; // 2. Allocate FIRST!
        std::copy(rhs.data_, rhs.data_ + rhs.size_, fresh);    // 3. Copy into fresh

        delete[] data_;       // 4. Free old buffer ONLY after new one succeeded!
        data_ = fresh;        // 5. Commit new pointer & size
        size_ = rhs.size_;
        return *this;         // 6. Enable chaining
    }

    ~IntBuffer() { delete[] data_; }
    std::size_t size() const { return size_; }
    int get(std::size_t i) const { return data_[i]; }
};
```

### Dry Run of `b = a;` and `a = a;`
- Suppose `a` owns `[10, 20]` (`size_=2, data_=0x100`) and `b` owns `[99]` (`size_=1, data_=0x200`).
- **Step 1 (`b = a`):** `this` is `&b`, `&rhs` is `&a`. `this != &rhs`.
- **Step 2:** `fresh = new int[2]` allocates `0x300`. If `new[]` throws `std::bad_alloc`, `b` still safely owns `0x200` (`[99]`).
- **Step 3:** Copies `[10, 20]` into `0x300`.
- **Step 4:** `delete[] 0x200` frees `b`'s old `[99]`.
- **Step 5–6:** `b.data_ = 0x300`, `b.size_ = 2`, returns `b&`.
- **Self-assignment (`a = a` or `ptr1 = ptr2` where `ptr1 == ptr2`):** `this == &rhs` triggers immediately and returns `*this` with zero allocation or corruption!

### Pattern 2: Copy-and-Swap Idiom (Cleanest Rule of 3/5 Integration)
```cpp
#include <utility>

class CleanBuffer {
    std::size_t size_;
    int* data_;
public:
    // ... constructor, copy constructor, destructor ...
    friend void swap(CleanBuffer& a, CleanBuffer& b) noexcept {
        std::swap(a.size_, b.size_);
        std::swap(a.data_, b.data_);
    }
    CleanBuffer& operator=(CleanBuffer rhs) noexcept { // Parameter copied before body!
        swap(*this, rhs);
        return *this;
    } // Old buffer inside 'rhs' is automatically deleted here!
};
```
*(In Rule of 0, using `std::vector<int>` handles all of this automatically!)*

---

## 5. 10 Theory Interview Questions (Copy Assignment)

1. **What is the difference between `MyClass b = a;` and `b = a;`?**  
   `MyClass b = a;` initializes a new object via the **copy constructor**; `b = a;` overwrites an existing object via the **copy assignment operator**.
2. **What happens if your class contains a `const` data member or a reference (`int&`)?**  
   The compiler marks the implicit `operator=` as **`= delete`** because `const` members and references cannot be rebound/overwritten after construction!
3. **Why is `if (this == &rhs) return *this;` important even if you never write `a = a;` explicitly?**  
   Self-assignment happens invisibly via aliases: `arr[i] = arr[j];` when `i == j`, or `*p = *q;` when both pointers point to the same object!
4. **Why must `new[]` happen *before* `delete[]` inside `operator=`?**  
   For **strong exception safety**: if `new[]` throws `std::bad_alloc`, deleting `data_` beforehand would leave the object holding a dangling pointer (`~T()` would later double-free!).
5. **Can `operator=` be overloaded for different right-hand types (e.g., `MyString& operator=(const char*)`)?**  
   Yes! Only `T& operator=(const T&)` is the *copy* assignment operator, while `operator=(const char*)` is a regular assignment overload.
6. **Can `operator=` be a non-member (`friend`) function?**  
   **No!** `operator=` is one of the 4 operators (`=`, `()`, `[]`, `->`) that C++ mandates **must** be non-static member functions.
7. **Does `Derived` inherit `Base::operator=`?**  
   Not cleanly: the compiler generates `Derived::operator=(const Derived&)` which hides `Base::operator=`, and if you write a custom `Derived::operator=`, you must explicitly call `Base::operator=(rhs);`.
8. **How do you forbid copying/assignment of a class in C++17?**  
   `MyClass(const MyClass&) = delete;` and `MyClass& operator=(const MyClass&) = delete;`.
9. **When does Copy-and-Swap have a minor performance drawback compared to manual `operator=`?**  
   When `*this` already owns a large capacity buffer (like `std::vector` with capacity 1000 assigning a size-5 vector): copy-and-swap always performs a fresh heap allocation instead of reusing existing buffer memory.
10. **What happens to the implicit copy assignment operator if you write a move constructor `T(T&&)`?**  
    The compiler implicitly **deletes** copy assignment (and copy constructor) unless you explicitly `= default` or define them!

---

## 6. 5 Output-Based Questions (Copy Assignment)

### Output Q1: Declaration vs Assignment + Chaining
```cpp
#include <iostream>
struct T {
    int v;
    T(int x = 0) : v(x) {}
    T(const T& o) : v(o.v) { std::cout << "CC "; }
    T& operator=(const T& r) { v = r.v; std::cout << "CA "; return *this; }
};
int main() {
    T a(1), b(2), c(3);
    T d = a;
    c = b = a;
    std::cout << c.v << d.v << "\n";
}
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer & Dry Run</summary>

- **Output:** `CC CA CA 11`
- **Dry Run:** `T d = a;` calls copy ctor (`CC `); `c = b = a;` runs `b = a` (`CA `, `b.v=1`), then `c = b` (`CA `, `c.v=1`). Prints `11`.
</details>

### Output Q2: Missing `Base::operator=(rhs)` in Derived!
```cpp
#include <iostream>
struct Base {
    int b;
    Base(int x = 0) : b(x) {}
    Base& operator=(const Base& r) { b = r.b; return *this; }
};
struct Derived : Base {
    int d;
    Derived(int x, int y) : Base(x), d(y) {}
    Derived& operator=(const Derived& r) {
        d = r.d; // Forgot Base::operator=(r)!
        return *this;
    }
};
int main() {
    Derived x(10, 20), y(99, 88);
    x = y;
    std::cout << x.b << " " << x.d << "\n";
}
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer & Dry Run</summary>

- **Output:** `10 88`
- **Dry Run:** Custom `Derived::operator=` overwrote `d` (`88`) but never called `Base::operator=(r)`, so `x.b` stays stale at `10`!
</details>

### Output Q3: Self-Assignment Without Guard (UB Warning!)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
struct Danger {
    int* p;
    Danger(int v) : p(new int(v)) {}
    Danger& operator=(const Danger& r) {
        delete p;            // BUG: No self-assignment check!
        p = new int(*r.p);   // Reads freed *r.p if &r == this -> UB!
        return *this;
    }
    ~Danger() { delete p; }
};
// Calling `Danger x(5); x = x;` causes Undefined Behavior!
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer & Explanation</summary>

- **Result:** **[UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR]** on `x = x;`. `delete p` frees `r.p` (same address!), then `*r.p` dereferences freed heap memory! Fix with `if (this == &r) return *this;` or allocate `new int(*r.p)` before `delete p`.
</details>

### Output Q4: Chained Modification via `T&` Return
```cpp
#include <iostream>
struct Num {
    int x;
    Num(int v = 0) : x(v) {}
    Num& operator=(const Num& r) { x = r.x; return *this; }
};
int main() {
    Num a(1), b(2), c(3);
    (a = b) = c;
    std::cout << a.x << " " << b.x << " " << c.x << "\n";
}
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer & Dry Run</summary>

- **Output:** `3 2 3`
- **Dry Run:** `(a = b)` sets `a.x = 2` and returns `a&`. Then `a& = c` sets `a.x = 3`! `b` remains `2`.
</details>

### Output Q5: `const` Member Deletes Implicit `operator=`
```cpp
struct FixedId {
    const int id;
    FixedId(int i) : id(i) {}
};
int main() {
    FixedId a(1), b(2);
    // a = b; // <-- What happens here?
}
```
<details>
<summary>Reveal Answer</summary>

- **Result:** **COMPILE ERROR** (`use of deleted function 'FixedId& FixedId::operator=(const FixedId&)'`). Because `id` is `const`, the compiler deletes implicit `operator=`.
</details>

